#!/bin/sh
# Runs the same battery as CI before letting a push through: `pnpm verify`.
#
# Why it calls only `pnpm verify`: one source of truth. Add a gate to that script and
# this hook follows. A hook with its own hardcoded list drifts from CI, and a drifting
# hook is worse than none — it teaches you that green locally means green in CI.
#
# Why not lint-staged: the failures this catches are whole-project by nature. knip
# looks for unused exports ACROSS files, and tsc is a whole-program checker, so a
# change in one file breaks another. Filtering to staged files would catch neither.
#
# Why not husky or lefthook: husky's function is `git config core.hooksPath`, which the
# `prepare` script does in one line. lefthook would parallelise, and the battery
# measures ~33s serial against an ~18s floor — less in practice, since `pnpm -r`
# already parallelises across the four packages and tsc and vitest contend for cores.
#
# Bypass deliberately with `git push --no-verify`.

echo "pre-push: running pnpm verify (about 35s; --no-verify to skip)"

if pnpm verify; then
  exit 0
fi

cat >&2 <<'HINT'

pre-push: verify failed, so the push was refused.

If the failure was `RangeError: Array buffer allocation failed` inside knip, that is
your MACHINE, not your code. knip 6 parses through oxc-parser, which reserves a single
ArrayBuffer of BLOCK_SIZE (2 GiB) + BLOCK_ALIGN (4 GiB) = 6 GiB. It is by design and
identical in the latest release, so upgrading does not help. A machine that cannot
allocate that cannot run knip at all.

In that case `git push --no-verify` is right: CI runs knip on a runner with enough
memory and stays authoritative. Do NOT remove knip from `verify` to work around a
local memory limit — that would weaken the gate for every machine that can run it.

HINT
exit 1
